# Workflow: planning and execution

`Tier 2 · Part of: [Platform](README.md)`

Question answered: How are accepted requirements turned into governed work and dependencies?

## Establish intent before planning

**M1 planning scope:** the fixed Compiler Intent + Requirement sequence and their required independent gates produce an accepted `research_brief`. The static template is the Phase 1 baseline. The targeted Phase 3 Planner proposes a bounded JSON DAG of downstream CCs with objective coverage, typed ports, dependencies and any suggested extra checks. The protected binder independently selects mandatory profiles, validates coverage, ports, dependencies, cycles, permissions and resource feasibility, assigns required checks, and freezes the [`run_plan`](../reference/run-plan.md) before research dispatch. An infeasible or rejected proposal halts the run and starts one linked new Intention Compiler attempt that carries the reasons. A request gets at most two plan attempts, then halts and writes an escalation record ([D30](../method/decisions.md#d30)). There is no silent switch to the static template. Both plan sources yield the same run plan type ([D26](../method/decisions.md#d26)). The run request sets `plan_source` before execution. The governed research journey remains Search → Screen → Hypothesis → Builder → Benchmark → Scientific Evaluation → Delivery.

**Required now.** Ordinary intake binds the original request and permitted documents, project assets, and validation data to a run. Reject empty or unreadable required input. Do not fetch undeclared datasets, clone arbitrary repositories, or turn missing evidence into observed facts.

D5 separates Intent and Requirement compilation, with independent verification at each boundary. The Delivery Phase 1 product entry remains bounded and non-interactive: one orchestration request produces the complete Brief without autonomous solution design. The current PRD explicitly leaves internal bounded, non-interactive compiler steps to architecture; their combined time/call limits are frozen. The earlier complete Intention Compiler node exercised only the first; the current complete compiler build must reach accepted Brief. The accepted Research Brief states objectives, scope, constraints, deliverables, and evidence obligations. Preserve ambiguity; a material unresolved requirement blocks readiness. The [Intent/Requirements field contract](../reference/intent-and-requirements.md) fixes shared information; protected default policy supplies only authorized missing parameters. A topic with no workable purpose/result halts rather than receiving invented intent. These fixed verification assignments cannot be removed by the planner.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    Intake[Intake] --> Intent
    subgraph Compiler[Intention Compiler node]
        Intent[Intent work subnode]
    Intent --> IV[Intent checks, verifier subnode and protected gate]
    IV --> Req[Requirements work subnode]
    Req --> RV[Requirements checks, verifier subnode and protected gate]
    RV --> NG[Protected node finalization]
    end
    NG --> Tpl[Plan source chosen before the run: static template or Planner node]
    Tpl --> Bind[Protected binder checks and binds one run_plan type]
    Bind --> PV[Plan gate: one decision. Planned: Planner node gate plus binder checks. Static: binder checks only]
    PV -->|rejected or infeasible| Halt0[Halt. A planned run then starts one linked new Intention Compiler attempt. A rejected static template ends with an escalation record]
    PV -->|accepted, stored as acceptance_ref| Freeze[Binder freezes run_plan]
    Freeze --> Run[Execute research DAG]
    Run --> Out[Expose verified delivery]
    subgraph Legend[Legend]
        Key[Blue: CC work; purple: verification; amber: infrastructure]
    end
    classDef work fill:#E8F0FE,stroke:#2563EB,color:#172554
    classDef verify fill:#F3E8FF,stroke:#7E22CE,color:#3B0764
    classDef control fill:#FEF3C7,stroke:#B45309,color:#451A03
    class Intent,Req,Tpl,Run work
    class IV,RV,PV verify
    class Intake,Bind,Freeze,Out,NG control
```

*Level: from intake to the frozen run plan. Omits: research nodes, check bundle detail and the failure branches in [failure and recovery](failure-and-recovery.md).*

Verification boxes include deterministic checks, a separate verifier invocation, and protected gate application, as specified in [capsules](../capability-capsules/library-and-checking.md). A clarification or blocking result is recorded through the control plane; headless evaluation returns a blocking status without waiting for interactive input.

Preparation is a protected fixed sequence established by the run-state module before the research graph exists. It gives each enclosing preparation node and its work/verifier subnodes distinct node/subnode/attempt identities, admitted pins, input references, policy and budgets. The same runner and release boundary apply; a pre-freeze invocation is not permission to execute a proposed graph. Freeze authorizes only the later research DAG. This prevents a circular dependency in which the planner would need its own finished graph before it could run.

The plan gate is one decision. For a planned run it combines the Planner node gate and the binder checks. For a static run it is the binder checks only, because the static template has no verifier ([plan gate](../reference/run-plan.md#plan-gate)). The [Checking](../checking/README.md) defines shared profile resolution for fixed preparation and planned work. The protected binder remains present in both phases; a planner supplies a proposal, never a replacement for binding or independent check assignment. Preparation templates are bound and checked before dispatch just as later contract templates are; research graph freeze is the later aggregate authorization.

## Planner choice and limits

The accepted Brief is produced first by fixed Compiler Intent and Requirement work with mandatory checks. The core planner then creates a bounded JSON DAG of admitted downstream CCs. Inputs include accepted requirements, a captured admitted-library snapshot, permitted resources, effective policy and limits. The proposal carries objective-to-result coverage, typed ports, dependencies, and optional additional checks. [Plan and node fields](../reference/field-catalog.md#planning-and-node-contracts) define the contract. The protected binder independently assigns mandatory profiles and checks, validates requirement coverage, typed-port compatibility, dependencies, cycles, permissions and resource feasibility, and freezes the accepted graph before dispatch. The planner cannot execute generated plan code, grant access, change active library versions, or waive required verification. Invalid or failed planning halts. The static template is the Phase 1 baseline, chosen before the run by the `plan_source` run setting ([D39](../method/decisions.md#d39)), not an automatic recovery path.

M1 Planner calls produce one `plan_proposal` each. The protected binder rejects missing or ineligible capabilities, selected CC versions without an explicit admitted check-bundle association and compatibility evidence, incompatible port meanings, cycles, unbound inputs, denied effects, conflicting shared writes, absent mandatory verification, and exceeded limits; it independently checks requirement coverage and known feasibility before freeze. Binding captures exact CC/bundle pins and adds contract/policy checks to each CC's required bundle; both sources are mandatory. The semantic model sees only the Planner declaration and exact plan output ([D24](../method/decisions.md#d24)). Coverage against the Brief is a binder check, not a semantic pass. See the [Planner page](../reference/capsules/planner.md). No available capability means infeasible planning, not an invitation to invent one.

Later work (November, [D21](../method/decisions.md#d21)): a search-based planner may generate several candidates and rank feasible ones by comparable evidence of successful outcomes, then time and cost. M1 has no candidate ranking.

This is the best candidate found within a declared search budget, not globally optimal planning. A separate logical-operator layer would add binding flexibility but also another representation to reconcile; direct CC bindings provide one inspectable execution boundary shared with the static template. Retain objective mappings so later logical planning can lower into that same boundary. Separating graph proposal, dependency dispatch and execution makes their evidence and authority independently inspectable.

## From available CC to invocation

The captured library snapshot is the planning-time view of eligible CC versions. A static template names versions from that view. The Planner sees the accepted Brief, the snapshot, permitted resources and plan policy, then proposes bindings. The protected binder checks the proposal independently and freezes exact CC and check-bundle pins into one `run_plan`. The library may change later, but the frozen pins do not.

At dispatch, the scheduler waits for predecessor gates, builds the node and subnode contracts from the frozen templates, and binds accepted input references. The CC runner resolves the exact pinned body and invokes it under those contracts. Agent CCs use the native agent/model path. Tool CCs run pinned code. Builder-generated POC code uses the separate confined executor. The runner records the invocation; checks, verifier assessment and the protected gate decide release.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    LIB[(Eligible library snapshot)] --> STATIC[Static template]
    LIB --> PLAN[Planner proposal]
    BRIEF[Accepted Brief] --> PLAN
    STATIC --> BINDER[Protected binder]
    PLAN --> BINDER
    BINDER -->|validate and pin| FROZEN[Frozen run_plan]
    FROZEN --> SWARM[SwarmFlow scheduler]
    SWARM -->|after predecessor gates| CONTRACT[Dispatch contracts and accepted inputs]
    CONTRACT --> RUNNER[CC runner resolves exact pins]
    RUNNER -->|agent CC| AGENT[Native agent and model path]
    RUNNER -->|tool CC| TOOL[Pinned tool code]
    RUNNER -->|generated POC| EXEC[Confined executor]
    AGENT --> OBS[Outputs and invocation observation]
    TOOL --> OBS
    EXEC --> OBS
    OBS --> CHECKS[Independent checks and verifier]
    CHECKS --> GATE[Protected gate and durable decision]
    GATE -->|advance| NEXT[Release accepted outputs]
    GATE -->|halt| RETAIN[Keep failure evidence]
```

This is an ownership and data-flow view, not a prescribed class diagram or private API. Exact fields remain in the [run plan](../reference/run-plan.md) and [Node and Subnode Execution Contracts](../reference/node-execution.md). The CC runner and SwarmFlow adapter reuse the pinned JiuwenSwarm/OpenJiuwen runtime where compatible; [native reuse evidence](../reference/native-reuse-evidence.json) records inspected interfaces, not runtime acceptance.

## Freeze and unknown future values

**Required now.** Freeze the accepted topology, exact CC and dependency versions, each participating CC's check-bundle version and compatibility evidence, verification profiles, policy, limits, and input references before planned execution. Store node and subnode contract templates and their hashes at freeze. At dispatch the scheduler builds the concrete Node and Subnode Execution Contracts from those templates. It binds future-input references to the accepted predecessor outputs and records the bound check plan and the contract identities before the attempt starts. No active-library or bundle change can alter an existing run, and no post-start contract/check-plan edit is permitted. Future producer outputs are typed references, not already-known values. Freeze does not claim that a hypothesis or measurement exists before its producer runs.

Choose capabilities whose declared input range covers the supported M1 research domain. Use one opportunity and one hypothesis path. The Hypothesis CC establishes and freezes the baseline, validation resource, measurement definitions, success/falsification boundaries, and protocol before POC generation or empirical execution. Downstream work may apply that protocol but cannot edit it after seeing results.

Evaluate preconditions with the same semantics at planning and dispatch. Known false conditions reject a plan; value-dependent conditions remain explicit dispatch obligations. An unsupported hypothesis, missing resource, or false dispatch precondition blocks the run. Do not silently choose another capsule, alter the graph, or create a new protocol. Immutable pins prevent version substitution. A run keeps its pins to the end: a suspension or revocation after freeze changes later snapshots only. To stop a running version at once, a human cancels the run. Multiple planning epochs remain a future possibility, not M1 behavior.

## Tasks, nodes, and verification

**Required now.** A task groups objectives. A node is a governed objective instance, not a CC call. At dispatch the scheduler builds its Node Execution Contract from the node template stored at freeze, the accepted inputs, admission metadata and policy. It binds input and output meaning, acceptance and proof obligations, resource and effect limits and exact CC versions. A node holds one or more work subnodes. A **work subnode** is one work CC plus its checks. M1 run plan nodes hold one work subnode, and the Intention Compiler node holds two. Later extra work CCs use more work subnodes in the same node. Each output gets one verifier CC call, governed by its own verifier subnode contract. The PRD permits broader capability sets, but arbitrary capsule member graphs and composite/merged CCs remain later work. Required invocation checks remain, and node-level aggregate verification authorizes successor nodes.


Builder and Research Benchmark are two separate nodes, each with its own node gate. Their boxes show the check bundle, which runs once over all outputs, and one verifier CC call per output. Each output then gets its own gate decision, and the node gate aggregates them. A mandatory check failure gives one halting decision and no verifier call. The Scientific Evaluation and Delivery node boxes stand for the same pattern, which is not drawn here. [Research nodes](../pipeline/stages.md) draws every node. Dependency arrows mean runner-mediated transfer of accepted artifacts, not direct calls that bypass gates. A verifier assessment comes from a separately governed verifier subnode; neither is the downstream work Node B. All participating invocation evidence is aggregated against the node contract. Only committed node acceptance releases successor nodes. Verifiers do not recursively require semantic verifiers.

## Scheduling and failure

Use the [failure and escalation policy](failure-and-recovery.md) as the single routing authority. The [research port table](../pipeline/README.md#research-path-and-ports) supplies all stage inputs and outputs.

**Required now.** Dispatch dependency-ready nodes only after their required predecessor gates have durably advanced. Reuse an equivalent native topological scheduler. M1 uses one active research run and a sequential research path. Future independent-node execution requires declared effects/resources, an explicit execution profile and separate validation; it is not introduced by D1.

A node failure stops that node and the nodes that depend on it. The run ends `partial` and independent nodes still run ([D49](../method/decisions.md#d49), [failure and recovery](failure-and-recovery.md)). A security, budget or persistence fault stops the whole run. M1 runs one node at a time. Preserve the outcome and evidence of the attempt in progress. Cancellation does not undo effects already performed. Default to zero automatic repair or replay. A human correction creates attributable new work and must pass verification; a changed graph or objective needs a new plan and freeze. Never overwrite a failed attempt.

Browser disconnection does not cancel execution. Restart preserves accepted results and marks interrupted attempts paused for inspection. No native cache entry can replace an authoritative gate decision or justify repeating an uncertain effect. The first build starts a fresh run rather than resuming in place.

Scientific rejection or inconclusive measurement is different from infrastructure failure. Valid evidence and correctly applied criteria can advance to Delivery, which discloses the result and its limitations. All report preparation happens inside the application; the control plane transfers the accepted artifacts.


## Concrete execution boundary

The [M1 build scope](../3-build-plan.md) records the static baseline, targeted Planner path and their common protected binder (D19).

The binder checks, binds pins and freezes the plan with its contract templates. At dispatch the scheduler builds the enclosing Node Execution Contract and each exact [Subnode Execution Contract](../reference/node-execution.md) from those templates. Participating bindings name admitted declaration/body/dependency pins, role, effective authority and per-call limits; parent node limits bound their aggregate; subnode limits narrow each assignment. Inputs carry named typed ports and exact refs, outputs name catalog contracts/versions and evidence obligations. The checking profile is pinned in the contract; the bound check plan references that finalized contract without a hash cycle. Missing input or unsupported authority blocks dispatch, never becomes a placeholder observed artifact.
# Failure handling and escalation

`Tier 2 · Part of: [Platform](README.md)`

Question answered: What happens when work blocks, faults, restarts or is cancelled?

**Decision.** A node failure stops that node and its dependents ([D49](../method/decisions.md#d49)). A protected stop outside a node halts the run. A run is unattended after intake: nothing waits for a person, and each failure writes an escalation for later review when storage is available ([D50](../method/decisions.md#d50)). A person never turns a failed mandatory check into an advancing verdict.

The **protected gate boundary** composes deterministic checks, a verifier CC assessment and a protected host decision. The verifier reports an assessment verdict. The gate interprets it under policy and controls advancement through durable run state and scheduler readiness. A producer or a model-generated "pass" never controls scheduling. See [vocabulary](../glossary.md) and [verification](../capability-capsules/library-and-checking.md).

```mermaid
flowchart TB
 G[Node gate or attempt outcome] -->|accepted| N[Dependents become eligible]
 G -->|failed or blocked| F[Node failed]
 F --> E[Escalation record written]
 F --> S[Dependents marked skipped, reason = failed node]
 N --> K[Ready independent nodes continue in Kahn order]
 S --> K
 K --> O{At run end, any failed node?}
 O -->|no| C[Run completed]
 O -->|yes| P[Run partial: accepted artifacts and evidence kept]
 X[Protected run-level stop] --> H[Halt run, contain active work, preserve evidence]
 H -->|when storage is available| E
```

*Level: failure scope for one run. Node failures preserve independent work. Protected run-level stops override dispatch. Omits subnodes, check details and intake clarification.*

Delivery depends on every research node, so a partial run produces no report. In M1 the research path is one chain, so a failure there stops everything after it.

## What happens on each event

| Event | Behavior | Escalation record |
|---|---|---|
| Empty or unreadable intake; unsupported domain or mandatory input | Reject at intake, before any work, with the reason | No. The submitting surface shows the input correction |
| Material ambiguity or conflict at intake | The Intention Compiler halts at intake. An `interactive` run may ask bounded clarification questions first. A `headless` run uses supplied answers only ([D48](../method/decisions.md#d48)) | Yes, on halt |
| Work fault, invalid artifact, mandatory semantic failure (`FAIL`) | Node failed, dependents `skipped`, independent nodes continue | Yes |
| Model unauthenticated or unavailable, missing dataset or dependency, timeout (`ENVIRONMENT_BLOCKED`) | Same. A demonstrated implementation defect stays `FAIL` | Yes |
| Insufficient evidence or unresolved verifier judgment (`INCONCLUSIVE`) | Same | Yes |
| Denied node-local effect or access, invocation budget exhaustion | Contain the invocation, fail its node and skip dependents. Independent nodes continue while platform isolation remains intact | Yes |
| Protected stop outside a node: library, plan, run-budget or security failure | Halt the run and contain active work. An isolation breach or inability to enforce the boundary is a run-level stop | Yes, if storage is available |
| Infeasible or rejected plan | Halt with a `plan_infeasible` outcome. A planned run starts one linked new Intention Compiler attempt that carries the reasons. A rejected static template halts at once. At most two plan attempts per request ([D30](../method/decisions.md#d30)) | Yes, on the final halt |
| Persistence failure before release | Block readiness, keep what evidence can be saved, no pass | Yes, if it can be written |
| `PASS_WITH_KNOWN_LIMITATIONS` with all mandatory checks met | Advance; carry warnings to the report | No |
| Valid scientific rejection or scientific inconclusive result | The verifier may pass; Delivery runs and reports the result | No |
| User cancels | Stop new dispatch, contain active work, keep effects and evidence | No |
| Browser disconnect | Execution continues on the server | No |
| Engine restart interrupts work | Keep accepted artifacts, mark interrupted attempts `paused`, no replay. Recovery is inspection plus a fresh run in M1 | Yes |

The escalation record is the halting node-scope gate decision plus the [PRD minimum gate result](../reference/checking.md#prd-minimum-gate-result) view with `routing_action` `ESCALATE_TO_HUMAN`. Inspection lists unreviewed escalations. A record names the run, node and attempt, verdict, failed obligations, known effects, evidence references and the required correction. It holds no hidden fixtures or credentials. Faults in the same attempt attach to one record.

## What a person may do afterwards

Read the escalation, repair the environment, correct the objective, supply permitted resources, or start fresh attributable work. Record the actor and the changed inputs. The failed run and its verdict stay unchanged. A new run records its link to the previous one, re-resolves configuration and pins, re-freezes its plan and performs every check. Nothing edits an accepted artifact in place. This resolves PRD section 4.6.4 "manually correcting the run" as a new linked run, consistent with D4.

## RSI workbench

A candidate score decline or compatibility rejection records evidence and feeds the bounded offline search. Query or resource exhaustion ends the session with a summary. A security violation, degraded fixture boundary, referee fault or evidence tampering stops the session and writes an escalation for the operator. Hidden cases and per-case final feedback are withheld from escalation content. Successful evaluation puts a candidate package in the admission inbox. A human runs the admission command and a human activates the version. Better scores never promote automatically.
# 1. The system

`Tier 1 · Part of: README · Serves: Knowledge and Standard`

AI4Research builds on the existing JiuwenSwarm/OpenJiuwen runtime and adds governed research control. These views show the trust boundary, components, run flow and data ownership. See the [platform page](platform/README.md) for runtime reuse constraints and [native reuse evidence](reference/native-reuse-evidence.json) for source observations.

## Trust boundary: control flow outside, work inside

The system is built around one boundary ([D42](method/decisions.md#d42)). The components named here are defined in [Components](#components).

- **Inside a CC: the work only.** A CC is a reusable module, like a tool, a skill, an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server or an A2A (agent-to-agent) agent. It takes typed inputs and returns typed outputs. It does not know which workflow or node it serves. So an author can write a CC without knowing the pipeline.
- **Outside every CC: all control flow.** Protected platform code orders the work, checks it and decides whether it advances. "Protected" means no CC can call into it, change it or skip it. Everything that can halt a run or admit a CC is protected: binder, scheduler, check runner, gate host and admission. The RSI referee is protected too, in its own container.
- **The declaration is the only interface.** Protected code reads it to bind, limit and check the CC. It grants nothing by itself.
- **RSI changes only what the CC allows.** `evolution.may_change` lists the only parts an RSI child may change. Missing or empty means nothing ([D34](method/decisions.md#d34)). Checks, gates, the referee and fixtures are outside every CC, so improving a CC never weakens how it is judged.

**Diagram T.** Level: trust zones. Not shown: individual components (diagram B).

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph P[Protected platform code: all control flow]
        BND[Binder] --> SCH[Scheduler] --> CHK[Check runner] --> GH[Gate host]
        ADM[Admission]
    end
    subgraph C[One CC: the work]
        DEC[Declaration]
        BODY[Body: prompts, code]
    end
    P -->|reads| DEC
    SCH -->|inputs, limits, permissions| BODY
    BODY -->|outputs only| CHK
    EX[Confined executor: generated code] -.->|own OS identity| BODY
    RSI[RSI workbench] -->|child changes only may_change| ADM
```

The verifier CC is also a CC, inside the boundary: it assesses, and only the gate host decides.

## Hot path and side systems

The **hot path** is the code a research run cannot work without. A **side system** is built in M1 but is **off the hot path**: a run works when it is absent, off or broken.

| Side system | How it stays off the hot path |
|---|---|
| Model router | Before each CC call, the model bridge asks `RouterPort`, the interface to the router, which model to use, and that model stays fixed for the call. With the router off or not installed, the bridge uses the fixed **Codex route**. If an enabled router fails, takes over 30 seconds to choose or names an unapproved model, the bridge uses the Codex route too: the **Codex fallback**, the only meaning of "fallback" here. `cc.router.enabled` is the one switch, allowed on only under an approved evaluation profile ([routing contract](reference/model-routing-contract.md)). |
| RSI workbench | Human-run, in its own container. Reads exported records; writes signed candidate versions to the admission inbox. A human admits and activates them. |
| Platform benchmark runner | An outside client in the sidecar container. Submits ordinary runs through the control API and scores their records. |

If a chosen model fails during a call, the node halts; nothing switches models mid-call. A Codex outage halts each node that calls a model, so runs cannot finish until Codex returns.

## Components

**Diagram B.** Level: components inside the app container, and who calls whom. Not shown: the steps of a run (diagram C), the side-system containers (diagram A).

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    API[Control API] --> BND[Binder and freeze]
    LIB[(Library)] --> BND
    BND -->|frozen run_plan| SCH[Scheduler]
    SCH -->|Node Execution Contract| RUN[CC runner]
    RUN --> BRG[Model bridge] --> RP{RouterPort}
    RP -->|default, or any router failure| CX[Codex route]
    RP -.->|IPC, when enabled| RT[API router process]
    RUN --> EX[Confined executor]
    RUN -->|outputs| CHK[Check runner]
    CHK -->|check results| GH[Gate host]
    SCH -->|after mandatory checks pass| VER[Verifier CC calls]
    VER --> GH
    GH -->|decisions, accepted outputs| ST[(Run store)]
    ST -->|node released| SCH
    ST --> XP[(Record export)]
    INB[(Admission inbox)] --> ADM[Admission command] --> LIB
```

| Component | Owns | Never |
|---|---|---|
| Control API | Authenticated submission, status, inspection, the record export | Starts work on inspection |
| Binder | Checks a plan, pins exact CC versions and their check bundles, freezes the `run_plan` | Lets a plan choose, drop or weaken a mandatory check |
| Scheduler | Starts each node once all its predecessors are done (Kahn order), builds each Node Execution Contract, creates verifier calls | Runs two nodes at once in M1 |
| CC runner | Runs one CC call within its limits and permissions; records what happened | Decides acceptance |
| Model bridge | The one path for every model call; asks `RouterPort` | Switches models during a call |
| Confined executor | Runs generated POC code under its own OS identity | Reaches credentials, run state or fixtures |
| Check runner | Runs the pinned check bundle | Lets the CC author change a check |
| Gate host | Writes every gate decision; releases outputs only after durable storage | Releases anything not stored |
| Run store | SQLite run state, immutable artifacts, append-only observations | Changes an accepted artifact |
| Library and admission | Admitted CC versions, their check bundles and standing | Admits without a human in M1 |

Detail: [platform](platform/README.md), [checking](checking/README.md).

## A run, node by node

A **node** is a unit of data flow. It takes accepted artifacts in and releases accepted artifacts out. An **artifact** is a typed record such as `intent_ir` or `research_brief`.

**Diagram C.** Level: nodes. Every box marked "node" holds the structure in diagram D. "Research nodes" stands for six nodes, drawn one by one in diagram F.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    I[Intake: request and files] --> IC[Intention Compiler node]
    IC -->|research_brief| S{plan_source, set before the run}
    S -->|static| T[Static template]
    S -->|planned| P[Planner node]
    T --> BG[Binder checks and plan gate]
    P -->|plan_proposal| BG
    BG -->|accepted| F[Frozen run_plan]
    BG -->|planned plan rejected| H[Halt; start a linked second Intention Compiler attempt, the last one allowed]
    BG -->|static template rejected| H2[Stop; recorded for human review]
    F --> R[Research nodes, six, in Kahn order]
    R --> D[Delivery node: exactly one in M1]
    D --> O[Report and output directory]
```

1. **Intake** qualifies the request and the files the user permits.
2. The **Intention Compiler** node (PRD 3.1 and 3.2) has two work CCs. The Intent CC states what the user means in the **Intent IR** (`intent_ir`), without choosing a solution. The Requirements CC turns accepted intent into the **Research Brief**: objectives, mandatory outcomes, scope, constraints, deliverables and evidence obligations.
3. The run setting `plan_source` picks the plan before the run ([D39](method/decisions.md#d39)). `static` (the default) uses the **static template**, a hand-written plan. With the Intention Compiler it is the PRD's Phase 1 Default DAG. `planned` uses the **Planner node**.
4. The **binder** checks the plan and binds it to exact CC versions and inputs. The **plan gate** is one decision: the binder's checks, plus the Planner node's gate in a planned run. The accepted plan is **frozen** into one `run_plan`. Nothing in it changes during the run.
5. **Research nodes** (PRD 3.3 to 3.8) and **Delivery** (PRD 3.9) run as in the [worked example](#worked-example).

### Inside a node

A node holds one or more **work subnodes**. A work subnode is one work CC call plus its check bundle. The node's objective, inputs, outputs, limits and exact CC versions are in its **Node Execution Contract** (PRD name), which the scheduler builds when it starts the node.

The PRD's **Evaluator Gate** is built in three parts ([D29](method/decisions.md#d29)):
1. **Check bundle** (PRD Tier 1): deterministic checks, run once over all of the work subnode's outputs.
2. **Verifier CC** (PRD Tier 2): one call per output, which assesses it against its CC's declaration. Each call is a **verifier subnode**.
3. **Gate decision**: written by the gate host, one per output, then one node gate decision.

**Diagram D.** Level: inside one node. It draws one work subnode and two of its k outputs. A node with several work subnodes repeats the inner box.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    In[Accepted inputs] --> W
    subgraph N[One node]
        subgraph U[One work subnode; repeated per work subnode]
            W[Work CC writes outputs 1..k] --> C[Check bundle runs once over all k outputs]
            C -->|any mandatory check fails: no verifier runs| GH[One halting gate decision for the work subnode]
            C -->|all pass| V1[Verifier CC on output 1]
            C -->|all pass| VK[Verifier CC on output k]
            V1 --> G1[Gate decision for output 1]
            VK --> GK[Gate decision for output k]
        end
        GH --> NG
        G1 --> NG{Node gate: every decision advances?}
        GK --> NG
    end
    NG -->|yes: store durably, then release| Out[Accepted outputs to the next nodes]
    NG -->|no| H[Stop this node; skip its dependents; keep evidence]
```

An output advances only when every mandatory check passed and the verifier's verdict is `PASS` or `PASS_WITH_KNOWN_LIMITATIONS`. Any other verdict stops the node and writes an escalation record for later human review. Nothing waits for a reply ([checking](checking/README.md)). A scientific negative is not a gate failure. If Scientific Evaluation correctly concludes that the hypothesis failed, its gate decision is `PASS`, and Delivery reports the negative result.

| Node | Work subnodes | Outputs, so verifier calls and output gate decisions |
|---|---|---|
| Intention Compiler | 2: Intent, then Requirements | 1 each |
| Planner | 1 | 1 |
| Search, Hypothesis, Scientific Evaluation | 1 each | 1 each |
| Screening, Builder, Delivery | 1 each | 2 each |
| Research Benchmark | 1 | 3 |

Each node also writes one node gate decision.

### The Planner

The Planner reads the accepted Brief, a library snapshot, the available resources and the **plan policy** (the operator's limits). In one model call it proposes the nodes, one CC each, the edges, inputs, limits and which Brief item each node covers. It is checked like any node. The binder then checks coverage, library membership, port types, producers, cycles, check bundles, policy limits and Delivery leaves. The Planner cannot choose or weaken a check. Detail: [Planner](reference/capsules/planner.md), [run plan](reference/run-plan.md).

**What the Planner adds in M1.** The M1 library has one CC per research step, and their ports chain only one way. So the Planner can hardly produce a graph other than the static one. In M1 it proves the mechanism and sets budgets, coverage and search topic hints. It chooses between real alternatives once the library holds CCs that can stand in for each other.

**Kahn order.** The binder orders the frozen graph with Kahn's algorithm; among ready nodes, the one listed first goes first. M1 runs one node at a time. A node is ready when every predecessor's node gate has advanced and been stored. Every leaf is a Delivery node. One Delivery node reports one selected opportunity. M1 selects one opportunity and tests one hypothesis, so it has exactly one Delivery leaf ([D28](method/decisions.md#d28)).

### Worked example

Request: "Can a learned sparse index cut retrieval latency on our local corpus without losing recall?"

**Diagram F.** Level: nodes, each holding diagram D. Edges are the real data dependencies. Solid arrows name the main artifact. The dotted arrow stands for all accepted artifacts Delivery reads.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    BR[(research_brief)] --> SE[search] -->|candidate_set| SC[screening]
    SC -->|opportunity_card| HY[hypothesis]
    HY -->|hypothesis_blueprint| BU[builder]
    HY -->|hypothesis_blueprint| BE[benchmark]
    BU -->|poc_manifest, poc_artifact_bundle| BE
    BE -->|benchmark_payload| EV[evaluation]
    HY -->|hypothesis_blueprint| EV
    EV -->|evaluation_verdict| DE[delivery]
    SE & SC & HY & BU & BE -.->|all accepted artifacts| DE
```

| Node | What it does |
|---|---|
| Search | Finds cited candidate ideas |
| Screening | Scores them and selects one opportunity |
| Hypothesis | Registers metrics and pass and fail thresholds before any code exists |
| Builder | Packages a bounded POC without running the trial |
| Research Benchmark | A `tool` CC. Runs the POC in the confined executor; measures baseline, then treatment, on the same data |
| Scientific Evaluation | Applies the registered thresholds and states the scientific verdict |
| Delivery | Writes the report with its limitations |

[Artifacts on every edge](pipeline/worked-example.md).

## State

- **Run state.** SQLite in the app container is the source of truth for every run, node, attempt and gate decision.
- **Artifacts** are immutable files, identified by hash. A correction is new linked work, never an edit.
- **Records** under `records/` are append-only: every CC call, model call, effect and check result. UI views are built from them, never a second copy.
- **The record export** is the only way side systems see run data, read-only.

Detail: [data and state ownership](pipeline/README.md#data-and-state-ownership), [derived records](reference/derived-records.md).

## When something fails

- A failed node stops its dependents, marked `skipped`. Independent nodes continue in Kahn order. The run ends `partial`, with evidence kept ([D49](method/decisions.md#d49)).
- Nothing is retried. The only automatic follow-up is one linked Intention Compiler attempt after an infeasible planned plan ([D30](method/decisions.md#d30)).
- Missing model access, data or checks stops that node. Storage failures and protected plan, library, run-budget or security stops halt the run.
- No run waits for a human mid-run ([D50](method/decisions.md#d50)). Closing the browser does not cancel a run. A restart pauses the interrupted attempt for a human.

Detail: [failure and recovery](platform/failure-and-recovery.md).

## PRD phases

All three PRD phases are part of M1. **Phase 1**, the governed baseline, is the static template, every gate and the fixed Codex route. It alone is the PRD's core M1 gate. **Phase 2**, Local-Isolated RSI Implementation, is the RSI workbench: built in M1, validation is reported separately. **Phase 3**, dynamic integration, includes the Planner (the PRD's targeted full implementation, integration and verification item), API model routing and the other items in [phases](method/phases.md). Each reports implementation, integration and verification separately. Experimental paths preserve the Phase 1 baseline and can be switched off ([D33](method/decisions.md#d33), [D40](method/decisions.md#d40)).

